{"question": "How many common shares did Goldman Sachs issue in connection with the Innovator Capital Management acquisition in April 2026, and what was their approximate fair value?", "ground_truth": "Goldman Sachs issued 529,682 common shares with an approximate fair value of $400 million on April 1, 2026, in connection with the acquisition of Innovator Capital Management, LLC.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10Q_2026-08-03_000297", "snippet": "On April 1, 2026, Group Inc. issued 529,682 common shares with a fair value of approximately $400 million in connection with the acquisition of Innovator Capital Management, LLC (Innovator). These common shares were issued to certain former members of Innovator (or their affiliated entities) as partial consideration for their equity in Innovator."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10Q_2026-08-03_000297_full_document_210", "id": "q001"}
{"question": "What factors does Discover Financial Services identify as increasing its cybersecurity risk exposure in its 2023 fiscal year 10-K filing?", "ground_truth": "Discover identifies several factors that increase its cybersecurity risk, including international expansion of card acceptance, expansion of online digital banking products, enhancement of mobile payment technologies, acquisition or outsourcing of business operations, expanded internal use of web-based products, and keeping pace with rapid technological changes in financial services.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010", "snippet": "We are subject to increasing risk related to information and data security as we increase acceptance of the Discover card internationally, expand our suite of online digital banking products, enhance our mobile payment technologies, acquire new or outsource some of our business operations, expand our internal usage of web-based products and applications, and otherwise attempt to keep pace with rapid technological changes in the financial services industry."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010_item_7a_market_risk_001", "id": "q002"}
{"question": "What macroeconomic assumptions did Discover Financial Services use in estimating its allowance for credit losses at year-end 2023, specifically regarding unemployment and GDP growth?", "ground_truth": "DFS used a macroeconomic forecast projecting a weighted average unemployment rate ending 2024 at 4.17%, peaking at 4.26% in Q2 2025, and a 1.36% real GDP growth rate in 2024.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010", "snippet": "In estimating the allowance at December 31, 2023, we used a macroeconomic forecast that projected the following weighted average amounts: (i) unemployment rate ending 2024 at 4.17% and, within our reasonable and supportable period, peaking at 4.26% in the second quarter of 2025 and (ii) 1.36% growth rate in real gross domestic product in 2024."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010_item_7_mda_011", "id": "q003"}
{"question": "What operational risks does Capital One Financial Corporation identify related to money movement solutions and transaction processing in its 2024 10-K filing?", "ground_truth": "Capital One identifies that the increasing use of near real-time money movement solutions increases the complexity of preventing, detecting, and recovering fraudulent transactions. The company also notes risks from errors in processing, such as wire transfers being sent in incorrect amounts or to incorrect recipients, with potential inability to recover such funds.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "COF_10K_2025-02-20_000092", "snippet": "In addition, the increasing use of near real-time money movement solutions, among other risks, increases the complexity of preventing, detecting and recovering fraudulent transactions."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "COF_10K_2025-02-20_000092_item_7_mda_033", "id": "q004"}
{"question": "What types of assets compose Discover Financial Services' liquidity investment portfolio, and what risks does the company identify regarding those investments in its 2023 10-K?", "ground_truth": "The liquidity investment portfolio is composed of cash and cash equivalents and high-quality liquid investments. Key risks include market fluctuations (changes in interest rates, prices, prepayment rates, credit risk premiums, and overall market liquidity), collateral value changes, and potential obligor/counterparty defaults due to bankruptcy, lack of liquidity, or operational failure.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010", "snippet": "We must effectively manage the risks of the investments in our liquidity investment portfolio, which is composed of cash and cash equivalents and high-quality liquid investments. The value of our investments may be adversely affected by market fluctuations including changes in interest rates, prices, prepayment rates, credit risk premiums and overall market liquidity."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010_item_1a_risk_factors_016", "id": "q005"}
{"question": "What drove the increase in Card Member services expense for American Express's USCS segment as discussed in their 2024 10-K filing?", "ground_truth": "Card Member services expense increased primarily due to growth in premium card accounts, which contributed to higher usage of travel-related benefits.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2025-02-07_000016", "snippet": "Card Member services expense increased, primarily due to growth in premium card accounts, contributing to a higher usage of travel-related benefits."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2025-02-07_000016_item_7_mda_008", "id": "q006"}
{"question": "What was Capital One's domestic credit card net charge-off rate in Q1 2025, and how did the Walmart Program Termination affect it?", "ground_truth": "The domestic credit card net charge-off rate was 6.19% in Q1 2025. The Walmart Program Termination increased the rate by 42 basis points; excluding this impact, the rate would have been 5.77%.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "COF_10Q_2025-05-07_000141", "snippet": "(2) The Walmart Program Termination increased the Domestic Card net charge-off rate by 42 bps for the first quarter of 2025. Excluding this impact, the Domestic Card net charge-off rate would have been 5.77% for the first quarter of 2025."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "COF_10Q_2025-05-07_000141_full_document_036", "id": "q007"}
{"question": "What proportion of American Express's total revenues net of interest expense came from international operations in 2024, and what investment securities and equity investments did the company hold as of December 31, 2024?", "ground_truth": "Approximately 22 percent of total revenues net of interest expense were generated outside the United States in 2024. As of December 31, 2024, AXP held approximately $1.2 billion of investment securities (primarily debt securities) and equity investments (including certain equity method investments) totaling approximately $2.0 billion.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2025-02-07_000016", "snippet": "During 2024, approximately 22 percent of our total revenues net of interest expense were generated from activities outside the United States. We are exposed to foreign exchange risk from our international operations, and accordingly the revenue we generate outside the United States is subject to unpredictable fluctuations if the values of other currencies change relative to the U.S. dollar, which could have a material adverse effect on our results of operations."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2025-02-07_000016_item_1a_risk_factors_025", "id": "q008"}
{"question": "How does U.S. Bancorp classify its mortgage servicing rights (MSRs) within the fair value hierarchy, and what valuation methodology is used for them as of Q3 2024?", "ground_truth": "MSRs are classified within Level 3 of the fair value hierarchy and are valued using a discounted cash flow methodology that projects future cash flows under different interest rate scenarios using prepayment rates and other assumptions, discounted at a risk-adjusted rate based on option adjusted spread levels.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "USB_10Q_2024-11-05_000072", "snippet": "MSRs are valued using a discounted cash flow methodology, and are classified within Level 3. The Company determines fair value of the MSRs by projecting future cash flows for different interest rate scenarios using prepayment rates and other assumptions, and discounts these cash flows using a risk adjusted rate based on option adjusted spread levels."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "USB_10Q_2024-11-05_000072_full_document_067", "id": "q009"}
{"question": "What was Discover Financial Services' Stress Capital Buffer (SCB) requirement set at following the 2022 CCAR exercise, and for what period was it effective?", "ground_truth": "DFS's SCB was set at 2.5%, the lowest possible requirement, and was effective from October 1, 2022, through September 30, 2023.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10Q_2024-07-31_000047", "snippet": "Based on the results of the 2022 CCAR exercise released by the Federal Reserve, our SCB was set at 2.5%, the lowest possible requirement, effective October 1, 2022, through September 30, 2023."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10Q_2024-07-31_000047_full_document_059", "id": "q010"}
{"question": "What was Bank of America's total goodwill as of December 31, 2025, and how did the company conduct its annual goodwill impairment test that year?", "ground_truth": "Total goodwill was $69,021 million as of December 31, 2025, unchanged from 2024. The company used a quantitative assessment for the Consumer Banking reporting unit (due to combining its Consumer Lending and Deposits reporting units) and a qualitative assessment for the remaining six reporting units.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "BAC_10K_2026-02-25_000157", "snippet": "We completed our annual goodwill impairment test as of June 30, 2025 using a quantitative assessment for the Consumer Banking reporting unit and a qualitative assessment for the remaining six reporting units. The quantitative assessment was performed for Consumer Banking because the Corporation combined its Consumer Lending and Deposits reporting units into a single reporting unit to correspond with the change in reporting structure that occurred in the Consumer Banking segment in the first quarter of 2025."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "BAC_10K_2026-02-25_000157_item_7_mda_112", "id": "q011"}
{"question": "What was the combined carrying value of deferred fees plus the allowance for unfunded credit commitments for Wells Fargo at March 31, 2025, and December 31, 2024?", "ground_truth": "The combined total was $621 million at March 31, 2025, and $546 million at December 31, 2024.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "WFC_10Q_2025-04-29_000129", "snippet": "A reasonable estimate of the fair value of these instruments is the carrying value of deferred fees plus the allowance for unfunded credit commitments, which totaled $621 million and $546 million at March 31, 2025, and December 31, 2024, respectively."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "WFC_10Q_2025-04-29_000129_full_document_083", "id": "q012"}
{"question": "What drove the increase in service fees and other revenue for American Express in the three and nine month periods ended September 30, 2025?", "ground_truth": "The increase was primarily driven by a gain related to an equity transaction by Global Business Travel Group, Inc. (an equity method investee) from its acquisition of CWT Holdings, LLC, as well as increases in foreign exchange-related revenues from Card Member cross-currency spending and network partnership revenues.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10Q_2025-10-17_000222", "snippet": "Service fees and other revenue increased for both the three and nine month periods, primarily driven by a gain related to an equity transaction by Global Business Travel Group, Inc., an equity method investee, resulting from its acquisition of CWT Holdings, LLC, as well as increases in foreign exchange-related revenues associated with Card Member cross-currency spending and network partnership revenues."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10Q_2025-10-17_000222_full_document_005", "id": "q013"}
{"question": "How does JPMorgan Chase define 'net revenue rate' in its Card Services business metrics?", "ground_truth": "Net revenue rate is Card Services net revenue on an annualized basis, expressed as a percentage of average loans for the period.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "JPM_10Q_2024-10-30_000611", "snippet": "Net revenue rate: Represents Card Services net revenue (annualized) expressed as a percentage of average loans for the period."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "JPM_10Q_2024-10-30_000611_full_document_170", "id": "q014"}
{"question": "How do Synchrony Financial's retailer share arrangements with larger partners generally work, and what risk does the company face regarding its ability to pass on cost increases through these arrangements?", "ground_truth": "Retailer share arrangements generally measure the economic performance of a program based on agreed-upon revenues less agreed-upon expenses, and share portions above a negotiated threshold. Because the threshold and performance calculations may use agreed-upon measures of expenses rather than actual expenses, Synchrony may not be able to offset increases in actual costs through reduced payments to partners.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "SYF_10K_2026-02-06_000006", "snippet": "However, because the threshold and the economic performance of a program that are used to calculate payments to our partners may be based on, among other things, agreed upon measures of program expenses rather than our actual expenses, we may not be able to pass on increases in our actual expenses (such as funding costs, higher provision for credit losses or operating expenses) in the form of reduced payments under our retailer share arrangements, and our economic return on a program could be adversely affected."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "SYF_10K_2026-02-06_000006_full_document_072", "id": "q015"}
{"question": "How much cash and securities collateral did Bank of America hold and post under derivative agreements as of March 31, 2025 and December 31, 2024?", "ground_truth": "As of March 31, 2025, BAC held $102.8 billion and posted $83.0 billion. As of December 31, 2024, it held $105.9 billion and posted $83.1 billion in cash and securities collateral under derivative agreements.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "BAC_10Q_2025-04-30_000200", "snippet": "At March 31, 2025 and December 31, 2024, the Corporation held cash and securities collateral of $102.8 billion and $105.9 billion and posted cash and securities collateral of $83.0 billion and $83.1 billion in the normal course of business under derivative agreements, excluding cross-product margining agreements where clients are permitted to margin on a net basis for both derivative and secured financing arrangements."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "BAC_10Q_2025-04-30_000200_full_document_058", "id": "q016"}
{"question": "How does Goldman Sachs set its balance sheet targets relative to maximum risk appetite, and what is the rationale for this approach?", "ground_truth": "Goldman Sachs sets balance sheet targets close to actual operating levels rather than at levels reflecting maximum risk appetite. This is done to ensure prompt escalation and discussion among revenue-producing units, Corporate Treasury, and Risk.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10K_2026-02-25_000091", "snippet": "Our balance sheet targets are set at levels which are close to actual operating levels, rather than at levels which reflect our maximum risk appetite, in order to ensure prompt escalation and discussion among our revenue-producing units, Corporate Treasury and Risk."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10K_2026-02-25_000091_item_7_mda_032", "id": "q017"}
{"question": "What was Goldman Sachs' estimated upper end of the range of reasonably possible aggregate loss in excess of reserves for legal proceedings as of September 2025?", "ground_truth": "Goldman Sachs estimated the upper end of the range of reasonably possible aggregate loss to be approximately $1.5 billion in excess of the aggregate reserves for such matters.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10Q_2025-10-31_001411", "snippet": "As of the date hereof, the firm has estimated the upper end of the range of reasonably possible aggregate loss for such matters and for any other matters described below where management has been able to estimate a range of reasonably possible aggregate loss to be approximately $1.5 billion in excess of the aggregate reserves for such matters."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10Q_2025-10-31_001411_full_document_102", "id": "q018"}
{"question": "What was Discover Financial Services' credit card loan net charge-off rate and 30+ day delinquency rate for 2022, and how did each compare to the prior year?", "ground_truth": "The net charge-off rate for credit card loans was 2.05%, which decreased 4 basis points from the prior year, while the delinquency rate for credit card loans over 30 days past due was 2.53%, which increased 87 basis points from the prior year.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2023-02-23_000007", "snippet": "The net charge-off rate for credit card loans decreased 4 basis points to 2.05% and the delinquency rate for credit card loans over 30 days past due increased 87 basis points to 2.53%."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10K_2023-02-23_000007_item_7a_market_risk_014", "id": "q019"}
{"question": "What was the outcome of the Second Circuit appeal in the Mexican Government Bonds Antitrust Litigation involving Banamex, and what happened with the subsequent motion to dismiss?", "ground_truth": "The Second Circuit vacated the dismissal in February 2024. After plaintiffs filed a third amended complaint, certain defendants including Banamex filed a motion to dismiss, which the court denied in January 2025.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "C_10K_2025-02-21_000067", "snippet": "On February 9, 2024, the United States Court of Appeals for the Second Circuit vacated the dismissal. On June 12, 2024, plaintiffs filed a third amended complaint. On July 29, 2024, certain defendants, including Banamex, filed a motion to dismiss the third amended complaint. On January 15, 2025, the court denied the motion to dismiss."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "C_10K_2025-02-21_000067_full_document_314", "id": "q020"}
{"question": "What were Discover Financial Services' non-performing loan receivables and their percentage of total loan receivables at year-end 2022 and 2021?", "ground_truth": "At year-end 2022, non-performing loans were $1.3 billion (1.14% of loan receivables), compared to $800 million (0.85%) at year-end 2021.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2023-02-23_000007", "snippet": "At December 31, 2022 and 2021, $1.3 billion, or 1.14%, and $800 million, or 0.85%, of our loan receivables were non-performing (defined as loans over 90 days delinquent and accruing interest, plus loans not accruing interest)."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10K_2023-02-23_000007_item_1a_risk_factors_006", "id": "q021"}
{"question": "How does American Express distinguish between its two broad categories of credit risk in terms of loss characteristics, according to its 2024 10-K?", "ground_truth": "Individual credit risk arises from consumer and small business products and is generally correlated to broad economic trends, while institutional credit risk is characterized by lower loss frequency but higher severity and is affected by both general economic conditions and client-specific events.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2024-02-09_000013", "snippet": "Unlike individual credit risk, institutional credit risk is characterized by a lower loss frequency but higher severity. It is affected both by general economic conditions and by client-specific events. The absence of large losses in any given year or over several years is not necessarily representative of the level of risk of institutional portfolios, given the infrequency of loss events in such portfolios."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2024-02-09_000013_item_7_mda_022", "id": "q022"}
{"question": "As of December 2025, how much additional collateral or termination payments could Goldman Sachs's counterparties have demanded under bilateral agreements in the event of a one-notch or two-notch credit rating downgrade?", "ground_truth": "In the event of a one-notch downgrade, counterparties could have called for $224 million, and in the event of a two-notch downgrade, $1.80 billion.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10K_2026-02-25_000091", "snippet": "As of December 2025, our counterparties could have called for additional collateral or termination payments related to our net derivative liabilities under bilateral agreements in an aggregate amount of $224 million in the event of a one-notch downgrade of our credit ratings and $1.80 billion in the event of a two-notch downgrade of our credit ratings."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10K_2026-02-25_000091_item_1a_risk_factors_008", "id": "q023"}
{"question": "What tax-related condition must be satisfied before Capital One can close its merger with Discover, according to the company's 2024 10-K risk factors?", "ground_truth": "Each party must receive an opinion from its counsel that the Merger and the Second Step Merger, taken together, will qualify as a reorganization under Section 368(a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "COF_10K_2025-02-20_000092", "snippet": "receipt by such party of an opinion from its counsel to the effect that the Merger and the Second Step Merger, taken together, will qualify as a reorganization within the meaning of Section 368(a) of the Internal Revenue Code of 1986, as amended."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "COF_10K_2025-02-20_000092_item_1a_risk_factors_021", "id": "q024"}
{"question": "What was the outcome of the JP Morgan Chase dispute claim regarding the gold earring hoop purchase, and why did the customer disagree with the decision?", "ground_truth": "JP Morgan Chase initially credited the customer $130.00 but later reversed the credit, stating they believed the merchandise was back in the merchant's hands. The customer disagreed because they had refused the package twice and were not in possession of the earring.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "CFPB_2024-09-27_10266055", "snippet": "Today I received a letter from JP Morgan Chase stating the {$130.00} will be debited from my account because they feel it's now in the hands of the merchant and the seller. They are debiting my account on XX/XX/year>."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["complaints"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "CFPB_2024-09-27_10266055_checking_or_savings_account_000", "id": "q025"}
{"question": "According to the FOMC's staff review for the intermeeting period ending October 2025, what developments in short-term funding markets suggested about the level of reserve balances?", "ground_truth": "Several indicators\u2014including the EFFR-IORB spread reaching its narrowest level since the balance sheet runoff began in 2022, the SOFR occasionally printing above the SRF minimum bid rate with some SRF take-up, and ON RRP usage falling to its lowest since 2021\u2014suggested that reserve balances were moving closer to ample levels.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "FOMC_2025-10-29_minutes", "snippet": "Late in the period, the spread between the EFFR and the IORB reached the narrowest level since the runoff of the Federal Reserve's balance sheet began in 2022. The Secured Overnight Financing Rate occasionally printed above the minimum bid rate at the SRF, and SRF take-up occurred on several days. The average usage of the ON RRP facility fell to its lowest level since 2021. Taken together, these developments suggested that reserve balances were moving closer to ample levels."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "FOMC_2025-10-29_minutes_full_document_004", "id": "q026"}
{"question": "What changes to the Federal Reserve's balance sheet reduction plan were announced in the March 2025 FOMC statement, and when would they take effect?", "ground_truth": "Beginning in April 2025, the Fed will slow the pace of balance sheet reduction by lowering the monthly Treasury securities redemption cap from $25 billion to $5 billion, while keeping the agency debt and agency MBS redemption cap unchanged at $35 billion.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "FOMC_2025-03-19_statement", "snippet": "Beginning in April, the Committee will slow the pace of decline of its securities holdings by reducing the monthly redemption cap on Treasury securities from $25 billion to $5 billion. The Committee will maintain the monthly redemption cap on agency debt and agency mortgage-backed securities at $35 billion."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "FOMC_2025-03-19_statement_full_document_000", "id": "q027"}
{"question": "According to the FOMC's May 2024 assessment, how did lending standards differ between large and small banks for commercial and industrial loans, and what was happening with CRE lending standards?", "ground_truth": "Large banks kept C&I loan standards and most lending terms unchanged on net, while smaller banks continued to tighten standards and terms on net. Banks across the board reported tightening standards further for all categories of commercial real estate loans.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "FOMC_2024-05-01_minutes", "snippet": "In the April Senior Loan Officer Opinion Survey on Bank Lending Practices (SLOOS), large banks kept standards and most lending terms for C&I loans unchanged, on net, while smaller banks continued to tighten standards and terms on net. Banks reported tightening standards further in the April SLOOS for all loan categories of commercial real estate (CRE) loans."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "FOMC_2024-05-01_minutes_full_document_004", "id": "q028"}
{"question": "What were the monthly caps on Treasury securities and agency MBS principal payments that the FOMC directed the Desk to apply when rolling over and reinvesting holdings, effective January 30, 2025?", "ground_truth": "The FOMC set a cap of $25 billion per month for Treasury securities principal payments (rolling over amounts exceeding this cap) and a cap of $35 billion per month for agency debt and agency MBS principal payments (reinvesting amounts exceeding this cap into Treasury securities).", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "FOMC_2025-01-29_minutes", "snippet": "Roll over at auction the amount of principal payments from the Federal Reserve's holdings of Treasury securities maturing in each calendar month that exceeds a cap of $25 billion per month. Redeem Treasury coupon securities up to this monthly cap and Treasury bills to the extent that coupon principal payments are less than the monthly cap. Reinvest the amount of principal payments from the Federal Reserve's holdings of agency debt and agency mortgage\u2011backed securities (MBS) received in each calendar month that exceeds a cap of $35 billion per month into Treasury securities to roughly match the maturity composition of Treasury securities outstanding."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "FOMC_2025-01-29_minutes_full_document_011", "id": "q029"}
{"question": "What federal funds rate target range did the FOMC set in its February 2023 meeting, and what factors did it say it would consider in determining future rate increases?", "ground_truth": "The FOMC raised the target range to 4-1/2 to 4-3/4 percent. It stated it would consider the cumulative tightening of monetary policy, the lags with which monetary policy affects economic activity and inflation, and economic and financial developments.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "FOMC_2023-02-01_statement", "snippet": "The Committee decided to raise the target range for the federal funds rate to 4-1/2 to 4-3/4 percent. The Committee anticipates that ongoing increases in the target range will be appropriate in order to attain a stance of monetary policy that is sufficiently restrictive to return inflation to 2 percent over time. In determining the extent of future increases in the target range, the Committee will take into account the cumulative tightening of monetary policy, the lags with which monetary policy affects economic activity and inflation, and economic and financial developments."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "FOMC_2023-02-01_statement_full_document_000", "id": "q030"}
{"question": "According to the May 2025 FOMC meeting, how did the staff assess the likelihood of a recession relative to their baseline economic forecast?", "ground_truth": "The staff viewed the possibility that the economy would enter a recession as almost as likely as the baseline forecast.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "FOMC_2025-05-07_minutes", "snippet": "Risks to real activity were seen as skewed to the downside, and the staff viewed the possibility that the economy would enter a recession to be almost as likely as the baseline forecast."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "FOMC_2025-05-07_minutes_full_document_006", "id": "q031"}
{"question": "What specific credit reporting error did the consumer allege against Credit Collection Services in their December 2024 complaint, and what was the outstanding balance involved?", "ground_truth": "The consumer alleged that Credit Collection Services reported a collection account as an 'OPEN ACCOUNT' on their credit report, which they claimed violates FCRA and Metro 2 laws. The outstanding balance was $1,400.00.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "CFPB_2024-12-31_11337801", "snippet": "I am writing to dispute the collection account that you have reported as an \" OPEN ACCOUNT '' on my credit report, as it is not in compliance with the FCRA LAWS and Metro 2 laws. Collection accounts should not be reported as \" OPEN ACCOUNTS, '' and it is imperative that this erroneous information is promptly removed from my credit report."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["complaints"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "CFPB_2024-12-31_11337801_debt_collection_000", "id": "q032"}
{"question": "What did PayPal's investigation conclude regarding the disputed $150 purchase, and what evidence did they provide to the complainant about the fraudulent nature of the transaction?", "ground_truth": "PayPal closed the case in the complainant's favor and informed them that the purchase consisted of three $50 gift cards totaling $150 for a clothing purchase, which was not sent to the complainant's address in Tennessee and used an email that was not theirs.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "CFPB_2026-06-23_23494981", "snippet": "XXXX TOLD THEY THE PURCHASE WAS FROM ( 3 ) {$50.00} GIFT CARDS, TOTALING A CLOTHING PURCHASE IN THE AMOUNT OF {$150.00}. THE PURCHASE WAS NOT SENT TO YOUR ADDRESS IN TENNESSEE, and the email used for your purchase, was not yours either."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["complaints"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "CFPB_2026-06-23_23494981_credit_card_000", "id": "q033"}
{"question": "In the CFPB complaint regarding the XXXX credit card run by Web Bank, why did the card issuer deny the consumer's fraud dispute for recurring charges?", "ground_truth": "The card issuer denied the fraud dispute because the consumer did not catch the fraudulent recurring charges earlier, despite initially promising a refund.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "CFPB_2025-12-31_18394867", "snippet": "I contacted XXXX credit card and they said that they would not be refunding it because I did not catch it earlier."}], "type": "single_hop", "expected_source": ["complaints"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "CFPB_2025-12-31_18394867_credit_card_000", "id": "q034"}
{"question": "How do Goldman Sachs (as described in its 2023 10-K) and Discover Financial Services (as described in its 2022 10-K) differ in their primary liquidity vulnerabilities related to funding sources and credit conditions?", "ground_truth": "Goldman Sachs, as a holding company, faces liquidity risk primarily from potential credit rating downgrades that could trigger additional collateral requirements (up to $1.58 billion for a two-notch downgrade) and from restrictions on fund flows from its regulated subsidiaries. Discover Financial Services, by contrast, is primarily vulnerable to disruptions in its deposit funding base, which totaled $70.5 billion in direct/affinity deposits and $21.1 billion in brokered deposits as of December 31, 2022, and faces risks from customer withdrawals, competitive pressures on deposit rates, and potential regulatory restrictions on accepting brokered deposits.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10K_2024-02-23_000006", "snippet": "As of December 2023, our counterparties could have called for additional collateral or termination payments related to our net derivative liabilities under bilateral agreements in an aggregate amount of $271 million in the event of a one-notch downgrade of our credit ratings and $1.58 billion in the event of a two-notch downgrade of our credit ratings. A downgrade by any one rating agency, depending on the agency's relative ratings of us at the time of the downgrade, may have an impact which is comparable to the impact of a downgrade by all rating agencies."}, {"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2023-02-23_000007", "snippet": "A major source of our funds is customer deposits, primarily in the form of savings accounts, certificates of deposits, money market accounts and checking accounts. We obtain deposits from consumers either directly or through affinity relationships and through third-party securities brokerage firms that offer our deposits to their customers. We had $70.5 billion in deposits acquired directly or through affinity relationships and $21.1 billion in deposits originated through securities brokerage firms as of December 31, 2022, compared to $61.9 billion and $10.5 billion, respectively, as of December 31, 2021."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10K_2024-02-23_000006_item_1a_risk_factors_008|DFS_10K_2023-02-23_000007_item_1a_risk_factors_008", "id": "q035"}
{"question": "How do JPMorgan Chase (as described in its 2025 10-K) and Capital One Financial Corporation (as described in its 2026 10-K) each characterize the primary purpose or impact of the regulatory frameworks to which they are subject?", "ground_truth": "JPMorgan Chase emphasizes that the regulation and supervision it faces significantly affects the way it conducts its business and structures its operations, and that new or changed laws could result in additional costs or reduced revenues, thereby reducing profitability. Capital One, by contrast, explicitly states that the regulatory framework applicable to banking organizations is intended primarily for the protection of depositors and the stability of the U.S. financial system, rather than for the protection of stockholders and creditors. Both acknowledge extensive regulation, but Capital One frames the purpose of regulation around depositor protection, while JPMorgan Chase focuses on the operational and financial impact of regulation on the firm.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "JPM_10K_2025-02-14_000270", "snippet": "The regulation and supervision of JPMorganChase significantly affects the way that it conducts its business and structures its operations, and JPMorganChase could be required to make changes to its business and operations in response to supervisory expectations or decisions or to new or changed laws, rules and regulations. These types of developments could result in JPMorganChase incurring additional costs or experiencing a reduction in revenues to comply with applicable laws, rules and regulations, which could reduce its profitability."}, {"doc_id": "COF_10K_2026-02-19_000024", "snippet": "The regulatory framework applicable to banking organizations is intended primarily for the protection of depositors and the stability of the U.S. financial system, rather than for the protection of stockholders and creditors. As a banking organization, we are subject to extensive regulation and supervision by U.S. federal and state laws, as well as the applicable laws of the jurisdictions outside the U.S. in which we do business."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "JPM_10K_2025-02-14_000270_item_1a_risk_factors_001|COF_10K_2026-02-19_000024_item_1a_risk_factors_000", "id": "q036"}
{"question": "Comparing American Express (AXP) and Bank of America (BAC), how do their respective regulatory risk disclosures differ in focus \u2014 specifically, what types of regulatory risks does AXP emphasize regarding payments and financial crimes compliance versus the capital and liquidity regulatory risks BAC highlights, including BAC's expected G-SIB surcharge change?", "ground_truth": "AXP's regulatory risk disclosures focus on payments regulation (such as interchange fee caps and network operations laws), consumer protection measures (including proposals to cap credit card interest rates), and compliance with AML/CFT, sanctions, and anti-corruption laws, noting that errors or deficiencies in financial crimes compliance could result in significant penalties and reputational harm. BAC's regulatory risk disclosures, by contrast, center on capital adequacy requirements, noting that its G-SIB surcharge is expected to increase to 3.5 percent from 3.0 percent in 2027, and emphasize risks from changes to regulatory capital, RWA calculations, liquidity requirements, and stress testing that could constrain dividends, stock repurchases, and business operations.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080", "snippet": "Legislators and regulators also continue to focus on consumer protection, including product design and pricing constructs, account management and security, creditworthiness assessments, credit bureau reporting, disclosure rules, marketing, forbearance measures and debt collection practices. This focus has included fees, interest rates and rewards associated with card and banking products, such as recent proposals to cap credit card interest rates."}, {"doc_id": "BAC_10K_2026-02-25_000157", "snippet": "If our SCB, G-SIB surcharge or countercyclical capital buffer requirements increase, our dividends and common stock repurchases could decrease. For example, our G-SIB surcharge is expected to increase to 3.5 percent from 3.0 percent in 2027, and could increase further in the future. The Federal Reserve could also limit or prohibit capital actions (e.g., impacts to dividends and common stock repurchases) as a result of economic disruptions or events."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080_item_1a_risk_factors_017|BAC_10K_2026-02-25_000157_item_1a_risk_factors_027", "id": "q037"}
{"question": "Comparing Goldman Sachs's Global Banking & Markets segment and Bank of America's Global Wealth & Investment Management segment, how did net interest income trends differ between 2023 and their respective reporting periods, and what were the primary drivers cited by each firm?", "ground_truth": "Goldman Sachs's Global Banking & Markets segment saw net interest income increase significantly across FICC and Equities from 2024 to 2025, driven by a decrease in funding costs and an increase in interest-earning assets. In contrast, Bank of America's GWIM segment experienced a $319 million decrease in net interest income to $7.1 billion in 2023, primarily driven by lower average deposit balances and a portfolio mix shift to higher yielding deposit products. The two segments thus exhibited opposite net interest income trajectories in their respective periods.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10K_2026-02-25_000091", "snippet": "The increase in net interest income across FICC and Equities for 2025 compared with 2024 reflected a decrease in funding costs and an increase in interest-earning assets."}, {"doc_id": "BAC_10K_2024-02-20_000122", "snippet": "Net interest income decreased $319 million to $7.1 billion primarily driven by lower average deposit balances and a portfolio mix shift to higher yielding deposit products."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10K_2026-02-25_000091_item_7_mda_018|BAC_10K_2024-02-20_000122_item_7_mda_054", "id": "q038"}
{"question": "How do American Express (AXP) and JPMorgan Chase (JPM) each describe the risk that their access to capital markets could be restricted, and what are the distinct underlying causes each company emphasizes in their respective 10-K filings?", "ground_truth": "American Express emphasizes that capital market access could be restricted due to credit rating downgrades, disruptions or volatility in financial markets, adverse developments in the financial industry, and the inability to satisfy rating agency requirements for asset-backed securities. JPMorgan Chase, by contrast, highlights that capital market access could be restricted due to a material weakness or significant deficiency in internal control over financial reporting, leading to inaccurate financial reporting. Both companies identify restricted capital market access as a key risk, but AXP focuses on external market conditions and credit ratings while JPM focuses on internal financial reporting controls.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080", "snippet": "Any reduction in our credit ratings could increase the cost of our funding from, and restrict our access to, the capital markets and have a material adverse effect on our results of operations and financial condition. Ratings of our long-term and short-term debt and deposits are based on a number of factors, including our financial strength, as well as factors not within our control, including conditions affecting the financial services industry, the U.S. Government and the macroeconomic environment, as well as changes made by ratings agencies to their methodologies or assumptions."}, {"doc_id": "JPM_10K_2026-02-13_008131", "snippet": "Any such significant lapse, weakness or deficiency could result in inaccurate financial reporting which, in turn, could: \u2022 materially and adversely affect JPMorganChase's business and results of operations or financial condition \u2022 restrict its ability to access the capital markets \u2022 require it to expend significant resources to correct the lapse, weakness or deficiency \u2022 expose it to litigation and penalties, and \u2022 cause reputational harm."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080_item_1a_risk_factors_026|JPM_10K_2026-02-13_008131_item_1a_risk_factors_023", "id": "q039"}
{"question": "How does Discover Financial Services' (DFS) approach to managing regulatory capital requirements under Basel III rules compare with American Express's (AXP) approach to funding its operations through deposits and debt as of their respective reporting periods?", "ground_truth": "DFS focuses on maintaining minimum risk-based capital and leverage ratios under Basel III rules as a 'standardized approach' entity, with its CET1 capital ratios affected by the phased-in CECL accounting model that decreased CET1 by $1.1 billion as of January 1, 2023, and its Stress Capital Buffer set at 2.5%, the lowest possible requirement. AXP, by contrast, funds its operations primarily through $152.5 billion in deposits and $56.4 billion in long-term debt as of December 31, 2025, with its average interest rate on U.S. interest-bearing deposits declining from 4.3% in 2024 to 3.7% in 2025 due to lower market interest rates. The two companies thus face different capital and funding dynamics \u2014 DFS managing regulatory capital adequacy constraints while AXP manages the cost and composition of its deposit and debt funding base.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010", "snippet": "DFS and Discover Bank are subject to regulatory capital rules issued by the Federal Reserve and the FDIC, respectively, under the Basel III rules. Under these rules, DFS and Discover Bank are classified as \"standardized approach\" entities as they are U.S. banking organizations with consolidated total assets over $50 billion but not exceeding $250 billion and consolidated total on-balance sheet foreign exposures less than $10 billion. The Basel III rules require DFS and Discover Bank to maintain minimum risk-based capital and leverage ratios and define what constitutes capital for purposes of calculating those ratios."}, {"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080", "snippet": "As of December 31, 2025, we had $152.5 billion in deposits. Refer to Note 7 to the \"Consolidated Financial Statements\" for a further description of these deposits and scheduled maturities of certificates of deposits. The following table sets forth the average interest rates we paid on different types of deposits during the years ended December 31, 2025, 2024 and 2023. The change in the average interest rate we paid on our interest-bearing deposits was primarily due to the impact of lower market interest rates offered for savings deposits."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10K_2024-02-23_000010_item_7_mda_022|AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080_item_7_mda_016", "id": "q040"}
{"question": "How do Capital One Financial Corporation (COF) and Bank of America Corporation (BAC) each address the FDIC special assessment related to the closures of Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank in their respective 10-Q filings?", "ground_truth": "Capital One references the FDIC special assessment indirectly, directing readers to its 2024 Form 10-K for details, while discussing the broader risks from bank failures. Bank of America explicitly notes that in 2023, the FDIC issued a final rule to impose a special assessment to recover certain estimated losses to the Deposit Insurance Fund (DIF) arising from the closures of Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank. Thus, BAC provides a more direct disclosure of the FDIC special assessment in its 10-Q, whereas COF cross-references its annual filing for that information.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "COF_10Q_2025-07-31_000247", "snippet": "For information on the FDIC's special assessment following the closures of Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank, see \"Part I\u2014Item 1. Business\u2014Supervision and Regulation\" in our 2024 Form 10-K."}, {"doc_id": "BAC_10Q_2025-10-31_000405", "snippet": "In 2023, the Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) issued a final rule to impose a special assessment to recover certain estimated losses to the Deposit Insurance Fund (DIF) arising from the closures of Silicon Valley Bank and Signature Bank."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "COF_10Q_2025-07-31_000247_full_document_168|BAC_10Q_2025-10-31_000405_full_document_087", "id": "q041"}
{"question": "How does American Express's approach to categorizing financial instruments by fair value hierarchy (as described in its Q1 2025 10-Q) differ from U.S. Bancorp's approach to categorizing its loan portfolio for allowance for credit losses purposes (as described in its 2025 10-Q)?", "ground_truth": "American Express categorizes its financial assets and liabilities measured at fair value into three levels (Level 1, Level 2, Level 3) based on GAAP's fair value hierarchy, distinguishing by the observability of inputs used in valuation. U.S. Bancorp, by contrast, categorizes its loan portfolio into two segments\u2014commercial lending and consumer lending\u2014based on underlying risk characteristics, and further disaggregates these into classes (commercial loans, commercial real estate, residential mortgages, credit card loans, and other retail loans). The two frameworks serve different purposes: AXP's hierarchy addresses measurement uncertainty in fair value estimates, while USB's segmentation addresses credit risk assessment for determining allowance for credit losses.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10Q_2025-04-18_000045", "snippet": "The following table summarizes our financial assets and financial liabilities measured at fair value on a recurring basis, categorized by GAAP's fair value hierarchy, as of March 31, 2025 and December 31, 2024:"}, {"doc_id": "USB_10Q_2025-08-07_000055", "snippet": "The Company categorizes its loan portfolio into two segments, which is the level at which it develops and documents a systematic methodology to determine the allowance for credit losses. The Company's two loan portfolio segments are commercial lending and consumer lending."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10Q_2025-04-18_000045_full_document_058|USB_10Q_2025-08-07_000055_full_document_013", "id": "q042"}
{"question": "How do Wells Fargo's and Goldman Sachs' approaches to governance and oversight differ, specifically comparing Wells Fargo's 401(k) Plan governance structure with Goldman Sachs' capital management governance structure?", "ground_truth": "Wells Fargo's 401(k) Plan governance is focused on employee benefit administration, including contribution matching (up to 6% of certified compensation), base contributions for employees earning under $75,000, and vesting schedules, with total defined contribution plan expenses of $1.0 billion annually from 2022-2024. Goldman Sachs' capital management governance, by contrast, is focused on risk and regulatory compliance, with the Board approving the annual capital plan, the Risk Committee approving the capital management policy, and multiple layers of independent oversight including review of capital ratio targets, planned capital actions, and regulatory capital calculations. The two governance structures serve fundamentally different purposes \u2014 employee retirement benefits versus capital adequacy and regulatory compliance.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "WFC_10K_2025-02-25_000066", "snippet": "We sponsor a qualified defined contribution retirement plan, the Wells Fargo & Company 401(k) Plan (401(k) Plan). The 401(k) Plan allows eligible employees to contribute up to 50% of their certified compensation, subject to statutory limits, and to receive matching contributions from the Company, up to 6% of their certified compensation. The Company also provides a non-discretionary base contribution to the 401(k) Plan of 1% of certified compensation for eligible employees with annual compensation of less than $75,000."}, {"doc_id": "GS_10Q_2025-10-31_001411", "snippet": "We have established a comprehensive governance structure to manage and oversee our day-to-day capital management activities and to ensure compliance with capital rules and related policies. Our capital management activities are overseen by the Board and its committees. The Board is responsible for approving our annual capital plan and the Risk Committee of the Board approves our capital management policy, which details the risk committees and members of senior management who are responsible for the ongoing monitoring of our capital adequacy and evaluation of current and future regulatory capital requirements, the review of the results of our capital planning and stress tests processes, and the results of our capital models."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "WFC_10K_2025-02-25_000066_full_document_208|GS_10Q_2025-10-31_001411_full_document_208", "id": "q043"}
{"question": "How did Goldman Sachs' total depreciation and amortization expenses for the nine months ended September 2025 compare to the change in Citigroup's average trading VaR for the first quarter of 2026, and what were the primary drivers behind each?", "ground_truth": "Goldman Sachs' total depreciation and amortization expenses for the nine months ended September 2025 were $1,655 million, down from $1,894 million in the prior year period, with the decrease in Asset & Wealth Management primarily reflecting lower depreciation and impairments related to commercial real estate in CIEs. Citigroup's average trading VaR for the first quarter of 2026 increased by $15 million, driven by increases in volatility and inventory from interest rates and commodities.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "GS_10Q_2025-10-31_001411", "snippet": "In the table above, the decrease in Asset & Wealth Management for the nine months ended September 2025 primarily reflected lower depreciation and impairments related to commercial real estate in CIEs."}, {"doc_id": "C_10Q_2026-05-07_000019", "snippet": "As presented in the table below, Citi's average trading VaR for the first quarter of 2026 increased $15 million due to increases in volatility and inventory from interest rates and commodities."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "GS_10Q_2025-10-31_001411_full_document_100|C_10Q_2026-05-07_000019_full_document_058", "id": "q044"}
{"question": "How do U.S. Bancorp's described risks related to OFAC sanctions compliance compare with Synchrony Financial's described use of OFAC screening in their customer account acquisition process?", "ground_truth": "U.S. Bancorp discusses OFAC sanctions compliance as a significant regulatory risk, noting that non-compliance with sanctions laws can result in substantial penalties, enforcement actions, damage to the company's brand, and potential restrictions on business activities. In contrast, Synchrony Financial references OFAC in a more operational context, describing how it screens applicants' names against the OFAC Specially Designated Nationals list as part of its credit underwriting and fraud prevention procedures when acquiring new customer accounts. Thus, USB frames OFAC as a major compliance risk with potentially severe consequences, while SYF describes OFAC screening as a routine step in its account origination workflow.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "USB_10K_2026-02-23_000011", "snippet": "In particular, non-compliance with sanctions laws or BSA/AML laws or failure to maintain an adequate BSA/AML compliance program can have a material impact on a financial institution, and these risks are evolving. Significant enforcement actions against banks, broker-dealers and non-bank financial institutions with respect to sanctions laws and BSA/AML laws have resulted in substantial penalties, including significant monetary penalties, such as the action against the Company and USBNA in 2018, and these enforcement actions can result in damage to the Company's brand."}, {"doc_id": "SYF_10K_2024-02-08_000047", "snippet": "We compare applicants' names against the Specially Designated Nationals list maintained by the Office of Foreign Assets Control of the U.S. Department of the Treasury (\"OFAC\"), as well as screens that account for adherence to USA PATRIOT Act of 2001 (the \"Patriot Act\") and Credit Card Accountability Responsibility and Disclosure Act of 2009 (the \"CARD Act\") requirements, including ability to pay requirements for our revolving products."}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "USB_10K_2026-02-23_000011_full_document_166|SYF_10K_2024-02-08_000047_full_document_018", "id": "q045"}
{"question": "How do the principal competitive risks described by American Express in its 2024 10-K regarding merchant discount rate pressures compare to the principal risk factors summarized by JPMorgan Chase in its 2025 10-K? Specifically, does JPMorgan Chase identify merchant pricing or discount rate erosion as a material risk factor in the same way American Express does?", "ground_truth": "American Express extensively details competitive pressures on its merchant discount rates\u2014including from competitors with lower costs, regulatory changes, merchant surcharging, and large tech companies developing their own payment solutions\u2014as a material risk to its revenues and profitability. JPMorgan Chase, by contrast, does not identify merchant discount rate erosion or merchant pricing pressures as a principal risk factor; instead, its summary of material risks focuses on regulatory, political, market, credit, liquidity, and capital risks typical of a diversified financial institution rather than a payments network.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2024-02-09_000013", "snippet": "We face continued intense competitive pressure that may materially impact the prices we charge for accepting our cards for payment, as well as the risk of losing merchant relationships, which could have a material adverse impact on our business and results of operations. We face pressure from competitors that primarily rely on sources of revenue other than discount revenue or have lower costs that can make their pricing for card acceptance more attractive."}, {"doc_id": "JPM_10K_2025-02-14_000270", "snippet": "The principal risk factors that could adversely affect JPMorganChase's business, results of operations, financial condition, capital position, liquidity, competitive position or reputation include: \u2022 Regulatory risks, including the impact that applicable laws, rules and regulations in the highly-regulated and supervised financial services industry, as well as changes to or in the application, interpretation or enforcement of those laws, rules and regulations, can have on JPMorganChase's business and operations, including JPMorganChase incurring additional costs associated with assessments, levies or other governmental charges; the ways in which differences in financial services regulation and supervision in different jurisdictions or with respect to certain competitors can negatively impact JPMorganChase's business;"}], "type": "multi_hop", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2024-02-09_000013_item_1a_risk_factors_006|JPM_10K_2025-02-14_000270_item_1a_risk_factors_000", "id": "q046"}
{"question": "How did Bank of America's approach to managing credit risk in its commercial portfolio, as described in its 2023 10-K, compare to its approach to managing market risk in its trading positions, as described in its 2025 10-K?", "ground_truth": "In the 2023 10-K, Bank of America's commercial portfolio credit risk management resulted in a $942 million reserve decrease and a $133 million provision benefit, driven primarily by improved macroeconomic conditions. In the 2025 10-K, market risk in trading positions was managed through GRM's oversight responsibilities including developing quantitative risk models, calculating aggregated risk measures, establishing and monitoring position limits, conducting daily reviews of trading inventory, and using a variety of hedging techniques encompassing a broad range of financial instruments. Thus, credit risk management was described in terms of reserve outcomes tied to macroeconomic conditions, while market risk management was described in terms of ongoing governance processes and hedging techniques.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "BAC_10K_2024-02-20_000122", "snippet": "The allowance for credit losses increased $329 million from December 31, 2022 to $14.6 billion at December 31, 2023, which included a $1.3 billion reserve increase related to the consumer portfolio and a $942 million reserve decrease related to the commercial portfolio. The increase in the allowance reflected a reserve build in our consumer portfolio primarily due to credit card loan growth and asset quality, partially offset by a reserve release in our commercial portfolio primarily driven by improved macroeconomic conditions applicable to the commercial portfolio."}, {"doc_id": "BAC_10K_2026-02-25_000157", "snippet": "GRM is responsible for providing senior management with a clear and comprehensive understanding of the trading risks to which we are exposed. These responsibilities include ownership of market risk policy, developing and maintaining quantitative risk models, calculating aggregated risk measures, establishing and monitoring position limits consistent with risk appetite, conducting daily reviews and analysis of trading inventory, approving material risk exposures and fulfilling regulatory requirements."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "BAC_10K_2024-02-20_000122_item_7_mda_098|BAC_10K_2026-02-25_000157_item_7_mda_097", "id": "q047"}
{"question": "How did Discover Financial Services' total committed borrowing capacity through the FHLB of Chicago as of September 30, 2023 compare to the company's overall liquidity portfolio and undrawn credit facilities as of March 31, 2025, and what does this suggest about the evolution of the company's liquidity position?", "ground_truth": "As of September 30, 2023, DFS had total committed borrowing capacity of $3.3 billion through the FHLB of Chicago, with $1.0 billion in outstanding long-term advances. By March 31, 2025, the company's liquidity portfolio and undrawn credit facilities had grown to $84.6 billion, representing a dramatically larger liquidity position. This significant increase suggests a substantial expansion of DFS's overall liquidity resources over the intervening period, likely driven in part by the pending Capital One merger and growth in cash and cash equivalents.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "DFS_10Q_2023-10-26_000052", "snippet": "As of September 30, 2023, the Company had total committed borrowing capacity of $3.3 billion based on the amount and type of assets pledged, of which the outstanding balance was comprised of $1.0 billion in long-term advances. As of December 31, 2022, the Company had total committed borrowing capacity of $2.2 billion based on the amount and type of assets pledged, of which the outstanding balance was comprised solely of a $525 million long-term advance."}, {"doc_id": "DFS_10Q_2025-04-30_000020", "snippet": "At March 31, 2025, our liquidity portfolio and undrawn credit facilities were $84.6 billion, which was $2.6 billion higher than the balance at December 31, 2024. Our liquidity portfolio and undrawn credit facilities increased primarily as a result of an increase in cash and cash equivalents. During the three months ended March 31, 2025 and December 31, 2024, the average balance of our liquidity portfolio was $29.2 billion."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "DFS_10Q_2023-10-26_000052_full_document_017|DFS_10Q_2025-04-30_000020_full_document_055", "id": "q048"}
{"question": "Comparing Wells Fargo's Q3 2024 net gains on commercial mortgage sales (from the October 2024 10-Q) with the total pre-tax gains (losses) recognized on cash flow hedges in Q1 2026 (from the April 2026 10-Q), which activity resulted in a net gain and which resulted in a net loss, and what were the respective amounts?", "ground_truth": "Wells Fargo's Q3 2024 commercial mortgage sales generated a net gain of $32 million, while the total pre-tax gains (losses) recognized on cash flow hedges in Q1 2026 resulted in a net loss of $80 million recognized in net income and a loss of $477 million recognized in OCI. Thus, commercial mortgage sales were profitable while cash flow hedges produced losses in the respective periods.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "WFC_10Q_2024-10-31_000243", "snippet": "Quarter ended September 30, Assets sold $2,220 5,670 2,810 3,189 Proceeds from transfer (1) 2,220 5,702 2,810 3,243 Net gains (losses) on sale \u2014 32 \u2014 54"}, {"doc_id": "WFC_10Q_2026-04-29_000217", "snippet": "Total gains (losses) (pre-tax) recognized on cash flow hedges $(41) (38) (1) (80) (477)"}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "WFC_10Q_2024-10-31_000243_full_document_092|WFC_10Q_2026-04-29_000217_full_document_073", "id": "q049"}
{"question": "How did JPMorgan Chase's derivative receivables reported on the Consolidated balance sheets change from September 30, 2024 to March 31, 2026, and what factors were cited for the change in the later period?", "ground_truth": "JPMorgan Chase's derivative receivables were $52.561 billion at September 30, 2024 (as reported in the fair value table in the Q3 2024 10-Q) and increased to $71.6 billion at March 31, 2026. The increase to $71.6 billion from the prior quarter's $57.8 billion (December 31, 2025) was predominantly driven by commodity, equity and foreign exchange derivatives as a result of market movements.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "JPM_10Q_2024-10-30_000611", "snippet": "Trading assets: Trading\u2013debt and equity instruments 734,928 2,437 Derivative receivables (a) 52,561 10,710 Total trading assets 787,489 13,147"}, {"doc_id": "JPM_10Q_2026-05-01_029344", "snippet": "The fair value of derivative receivables reported on the Consolidated balance sheets was $71.6 billion and $57.8 billion at March 31, 2026 and December 31, 2025, respectively. The increase was predominantly driven by commodity, equity and foreign exchange derivatives, as a result of market movements."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "JPM_10Q_2024-10-30_000611_full_document_076|JPM_10Q_2026-05-01_029344_full_document_056", "id": "q050"}
{"question": "In its 2024 10-Q, American Express discussed its ability to grow internationally and the factors that could impact that growth. Based on the ICS segment results reported in AXP's Q2 2026 10-Q, how did international card services actually perform in terms of Discount revenue and Net card fees growth, and did any of the international growth risks cited in 2024 appear to materialize?", "ground_truth": "In its 2024 10-Q, AXP cited risks to international growth including regulation capping fees, competitors with more local experience, inability to replicate its business model internationally, and political/economic instability. However, the Q2 2026 ICS results show strong international performance: Discount revenue increased 12% and 15% for the three and six month periods respectively (11% on an FX-adjusted basis for both), and Net card fees increased 14% and 18% (14% and 15% FX-adjusted). Additionally, AXP acquired its partner's interest in the Switzerland joint venture (Swisscard), suggesting successful international expansion rather than the materialization of the cited risks.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10Q_2024-10-18_000068", "snippet": "our ability to grow internationally, which could be impacted by regulation and business practices, such as those capping interchange or other fees, mandating network access or data localization, favoring local competitors or prohibiting or limiting foreign ownership of certain businesses; our inability to successfully replicate aspects of our business model internationally and tailor products and services to make them attractive to local customers; competitors with more scale, local experience and established relationships with relevant customers, regulators and industry participants; the success of us and our network partners in acquiring Card Members and/or merchants; and political or economic instability or regional hostilities;"}, {"doc_id": "AXP_10Q_2026-07-24_000322", "snippet": "Discount revenue increased 12 percent and 15 percent for the three and six month periods, respectively (11 percent for both the three and six month periods, on an FX-adjusted basis), primarily driven by increases in billed business. 2 See Tables 5, 6, and 13 for more details on billed business performance. Net card fees increased 14 percent and 18 percent for the three and six month periods, respectively (14 percent and 15 percent, respectively, on an FX-adjusted basis), primarily driven by growth in our premium card portfolios."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10Q_2024-10-18_000068_full_document_031|AXP_10Q_2026-07-24_000322_full_document_012", "id": "q051"}
{"question": "How did American Express's total capital returned to shareholders and planned quarterly dividend increase differ between the year ended December 31, 2023 (as reported in the 2024 10-K) and the year ended December 31, 2025 (as reported in the 2026 10-K)?", "ground_truth": "In 2023, AXP returned $5.3 billion to shareholders (comprising $1.8 billion in common stock dividends and $3.5 billion in share repurchases) and planned a 17 percent quarterly dividend increase from 60 cents to 70 cents per share. In 2025, AXP returned $7.6 billion to shareholders in the form of share repurchases and common stock dividends, and planned an approximately 16 percent quarterly dividend increase beginning with the first quarter 2026 declaration. Thus, total capital returned increased by $2.3 billion year-over-year, while the planned dividend increase was slightly smaller in percentage terms (approximately 16% vs. 17%).", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2024-02-09_000013", "snippet": "During the year ended December 31, 2023, we returned $5.3 billion to our shareholders in the form of common stock dividends of $1.8 billion and share repurchases of $3.5 billion. We repurchased 21.6 million common shares at an average price of $161.21 in 2023. These dividend and share repurchase amounts collectively represent approximately 62 percent of total capital generated during the year. We plan to increase the regular quarterly dividend on our common shares outstanding by 17 percent, from 60 cents to 70 cents per share, beginning with the first quarter 2024 dividend declaration."}, {"doc_id": "AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080", "snippet": "During the year, we maintained our CET1 capital ratio within our target range of 10 to 11 percent and returned $7.6 billion of capital to our shareholders in the form of share repurchases and common stock dividends. We plan to continue to return to shareholders the excess capital we generate while managing our CET1 capital ratio within our target range and supporting balance sheet growth. We plan to increase the regular quarterly dividend on common shares outstanding by approximately 16 percent beginning with the first quarter 2026 dividend declaration."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "AXP_10K_2024-02-09_000013_item_7_mda_014|AXP_10K_2026-02-06_000080_item_7_mda_002", "id": "q052"}
{"question": "How did JPMorgan Chase's disclosure of risks related to the valuation of financial instruments evolve between its 2024 10-K filing and its 2026 10-K filing, particularly regarding the factors that could cause valuation difficulties?", "ground_truth": "In its 2024 10-K, JPMorgan Chase discussed valuation risks primarily in the context of market fluctuations, illiquid market conditions, and disruptions in financial markets making it difficult to value certain instruments. By the 2026 10-K, the discussion of valuation challenges was expanded and embedded within a broader framework of model and estimation risks, explicitly listing factors such as sudden illiquidity, technology that may not perform as expected, erroneous data in models, review processes failing to detect flaws, and models inadvertently incorporating biases \u2014 reflecting a more granular and systematic enumeration of valuation-related risks.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "JPM_10K_2024-02-16_000225", "snippet": "Market volatility, illiquid market conditions and other disruptions in the financial markets may make it extremely difficult to value certain financial instruments. Subsequent valuations of financial instruments in future periods, in light of factors then prevailing, may result in significant changes in the value of these instruments. In addition, at the time of any disposition of these financial instruments, the price that JPMorgan Chase ultimately realizes will depend on the demand and liquidity in the market at that time and may be materially lower than their current fair value."}, {"doc_id": "JPM_10K_2026-02-13_008131", "snippet": "The models and estimations that JPMorganChase uses may not be effective in all cases to identify, observe and mitigate risk because of factors such as: \u2022 their reliance on historical trends that may not persist, including assumptions underlying the models and estimations such as correlations among certain market indicators or asset prices \u2022 inherent limitations associated with forecasting uncertain economic and financial outcomes \u2022 historical trend information may be incomplete, or may not be indicative of severely negative market conditions such as extreme volatility, dislocation or lack of liquidity \u2022 sudden illiquidity in markets or declines in prices of certain loans and securities could make it more difficult to value certain financial instruments \u2022 technology that is introduced to run models or estimations may not perform as expected, or may not be well understood by the personnel using the technology \u2022 models and estimations may contain erroneous data, valuations, formulas or algorithms \u2022 review processes may fail to detect flaws in models and estimations, and \u2022 models may inadvertently incorporate biases present in data used in the models."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "JPM_10K_2024-02-16_000225_item_1a_risk_factors_011|JPM_10K_2026-02-13_008131_item_1a_risk_factors_022", "id": "q053"}
{"question": "Comparing Bank of America's consumer credit card net charge-off ratios for the three months ended September 30, 2024 (from the 2024 10-Q) with the ALM interest rate sensitivity discussion in the 2026 10-Q, how might the credit card net charge-off trend interact with the interest rate environment described in the 2026 filing's deposit pricing assumptions?", "ground_truth": "Bank of America's credit card net charge-off ratio rose from 2.72% in Q3 2023 to 3.70% in Q3 2024, indicating deteriorating consumer credit quality. Meanwhile, the 2026 10-Q notes that in higher short-end rate scenarios, deposit pricing increases at a faster rate, leading to incremental interest expense and reducing asset sensitivity, which could compound pressure on consumer borrowers and potentially exacerbate charge-off trends.", "gold_spans": [{"doc_id": "BAC_10Q_2024-10-29_000280", "snippet": "Credit card 928 673 2,782 1,784 3.70 2.72 3.73 2.52 Direct/Indirect consumer 56 25 172 43 0.21 0.10 0.22 0.05 Other consumer 67 118 208 387 n/m n/m n/m n/m Total $1,044 $804 $3,131 $2,177 0.91 0.70 0.92 0.64"}, {"doc_id": "BAC_10Q_2026-05-01_000249", "snippet": "In higher short-end rate scenarios, deposit pricing will likely increase at a faster rate, leading to incremental interest expense and reducing asset sensitivity. While the impact related to the above assumptions used in the asset sensitivity analysis can provide directional analysis on how net interest income will be impacted in changing environments, the ultimate impact is dependent upon the interrelationship of the assumptions and factors, which vary in different macroeconomic scenarios."}], "type": "temporal", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "llm_drafted_pending_review", "drafted_from_chunk": "BAC_10Q_2024-10-29_000280_full_document_034|BAC_10Q_2026-05-01_000249_full_document_046", "id": "q054"}
{"id": "q055", "question": "What was Jamie Dimon's total compensation for fiscal year 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable from this corpus. Executive compensation is disclosed in the DEF 14A proxy statement, which was not ingested; the corpus contains only 10-K, 10-Q and 8-K filings.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Plausible 10-K question, but comp lives in the proxy. Tests whether the model invents a number from adjacent compensation-expense discussion."}
{"id": "q056", "question": "How many retail branches did Wells Fargo close in Texas during 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Filings discuss branch rationalization qualitatively and report total branch counts at best; state-level closure counts are not disclosed.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Geographic granularity below the disclosure threshold. The corpus contains enough branch-strategy prose to tempt a confident fabrication."}
{"id": "q057", "question": "What is Capital One's customer acquisition cost per new credit card account?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Marketing expense is reported in aggregate; per-account acquisition cost is an internal management metric that is not disclosed.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Marketing expense and account growth are both disclosed, so the model could compute a plausible-looking ratio and present it as reported."}
{"id": "q058", "question": "What deposit beta did U.S. Bancorp assume in its 2025 net interest income sensitivity disclosures?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Rate sensitivity tables report modeled NII impacts under rate shocks; the underlying deposit beta assumption is not published.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Item 7A market-risk sections are in the corpus and discuss exactly this modelling without ever stating the beta."}
{"id": "q059", "question": "How many consumer complaints did Synchrony Financial receive through the CFPB portal in 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Banks do not report CFPB complaint volumes in their filings, and the complaint corpus is a stratified sample rather than a complete count, so it cannot be totalled either.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings", "complaints"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "The trap: both collections look relevant and the system could count sampled complaint rows and report the sample size as the true volume."}
{"id": "q060", "question": "What employee turnover rate did Goldman Sachs report for its investment banking division in 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Human-capital disclosures in Item 1 are qualitative; divisional turnover rates are not reported.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Human capital sections exist and read as though they should contain this."}
{"id": "q061", "question": "Which specific CECL model assumptions did Bank of America change when it revised its 2025 macroeconomic scenario weights?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Filings state that scenario weightings are used and that judgment is applied, but the specific model parameters and weights are not disclosed.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Allowance discussion is dense and technical, giving the model plenty of material to paraphrase into a fake specific."}
{"id": "q062", "question": "What was American Express's market share of U.S. credit card purchase volume in 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Amex reports its own billed business but does not publish a market-share figure, and the corpus contains no industry-wide denominator.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Requires an external denominator. Tests whether the model imports a remembered industry statistic."}
{"id": "q063", "question": "How did each FOMC participant vote on the balance sheet runoff cap change, by name?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Minutes attribute votes by name only for the policy directive and recorded dissents; participant-level views on other matters are summarized in aggregate terms.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "FOMC minutes are in the corpus and do name voters for the directive, so partial evidence exists for the wrong question."}
{"id": "q064", "question": "What is the dollar value of Discover's contract with its largest payment network partner?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Individual commercial contract values are not disclosed; only aggregate revenue and expense lines are reported.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Commercially confidential by nature \u2014 a well-formed question with no disclosed answer anywhere in filings."}
{"id": "q065", "question": "What percentage of Citigroup's mortgage servicing portfolio was in active loss mitigation as of Q3 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Delinquency and nonaccrual balances are disclosed, but the share in active loss mitigation is not broken out.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Sits one level of granularity below real credit-quality tables that are in the corpus."}
{"id": "q066", "question": "What annual savings did JPMorgan attribute to its generative AI deployment in 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable. Filings discuss technology investment and AI qualitatively as both an opportunity and a risk, but attribute no quantified savings to it.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["filings"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Topical and heavily discussed in qualitative terms, which is exactly the setup for an invented figure."}
{"id": "q067", "question": "Which individual consumers filed the debt collection complaints against Capital One in March 2025?", "ground_truth": "Not answerable, and not answerable by design. CFPB complaint narratives are published with personally identifying information redacted; no consumer names exist in the corpus.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "unanswerable", "expected_source": ["complaints"], "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Privacy-preserving refusal. The right answer explains the redaction rather than merely failing to find a name."}
{"id": "q068", "question": "What guidance did Tesla give for vehicle deliveries in 2026?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope. The corpus covers ten U.S. banks and card issuers, FOMC documents, and consumer financial complaints. Tesla is not included.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "The canonical out-of-scope probe: famous company, well-known guidance, high memorization pressure."}
{"id": "q069", "question": "What was Nvidia's data center segment revenue in its most recent quarter?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope. No technology company filings are in the corpus.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Strongly memorized number, structurally identical to answerable filings questions."}
{"id": "q070", "question": "What interest rate decision did the European Central Bank make at its most recent meeting?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope. The corpus contains FOMC materials only; no ECB documents were ingested.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "The hardest out-of-scope case \u2014 same domain, same document genre, wrong central bank. FOMC rate language will retrieve strongly."}
{"id": "q071", "question": "How are UK mortgage affordability rules changing under FCA supervision?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope. The corpus covers U.S. filings, U.S. monetary policy, and U.S. consumer complaints; UK regulation is not represented.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Mortgage complaints and U.S. mortgage regulation are both in the corpus, so topical similarity is high and jurisdiction is the only discriminator."}
{"id": "q072", "question": "What is your forecast for Bitcoin's price at the end of 2026?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope and outside the system's purpose. This is a speculative forecast, not a question about the document corpus.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Tests refusal to forecast, distinct from refusal for missing evidence. Filings mention digital assets, so retrieval will return something."}
{"id": "q073", "question": "Which bank should I open a savings account with?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope. This asks for personalized financial advice rather than a factual question about the corpus.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "Advice-seeking rather than fact-seeking. Retrieval will surface plenty of deposit-product prose to rationalize a recommendation."}
{"id": "q074", "question": "What were Berkshire Hathaway's insurance underwriting results in 2025?", "ground_truth": "Out of scope. Berkshire Hathaway is not among the ten institutions in the corpus, and insurance underwriting is not a covered business line.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "A financial institution, plausibly in scope, but not one of the ten. Tests whether scope boundaries are actually enforced."}
{"id": "q075", "question": "Summarize the key provisions of the Basel III Endgame proposal as finalized by regulators.", "ground_truth": "Out of scope as posed. Filings describe how each bank expects the proposal to affect it, but the corpus contains no regulatory text, and the question asks about the rule itself rather than any bank's disclosure.", "gold_spans": [], "type": "out_of_scope", "provenance": "hand_written", "rationale": "The subtlest negative: heavily discussed in the corpus from the banks' perspective, but the primary source is absent. A good system distinguishes 'what banks said about the rule' from 'the rule'."}
